---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="About Rev. Sabato Morais and the Sabato Morais Digital Repository."
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 sm:py-20">
    <header class="mb-10 text-center">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-3">About the Author</p>
      <h1 class="font-heading text-4xl leading-tight text-navy sm:text-5xl">
        Rev. Sabato Morais
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-2 font-heading text-lg italic text-burgundy">1823&ndash;1897</p>
      <div class="ornament mx-auto mt-6 max-w-sm">
        <span class="ornament-mark">&#10087;</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="prose prose-neutral max-w-none font-body text-lg text-ink prose-headings:font-heading prose-headings:text-navy prose-a:text-burgundy prose-strong:text-ink">
      <p>
        Sabato Morais was born in Livorno (Leghorn), Italy, in 1823. After a period
        in London, he was appointed hazzan and minister of
        <strong>Congregation Mikveh Israel</strong> in Philadelphia in 1851, a
        pulpit he held until his death in 1897. Over nearly half a century he became
        one of the most influential Jewish leaders in nineteenth-century America — a
        preacher, teacher, translator, and public voice on the religious and civic
        questions of his day.
      </p>

      <p>
        A committed traditionalist, Morais was a leading opponent of the growing
        Reform movement. In 1886 he helped found, and became the first president of,
        the <strong>Jewish Theological Seminary of America</strong>, established to
        train rabbis in the traditional practice and study of Judaism. His sermons,
        correspondence, and manuscripts document the intellectual and communal life
        of American Jewry during a formative era.
      </p>

      <h2 id="repository">About the Repository</h2>

      <p>
        The <strong>Sabato Morais Digital Repository</strong> gathers digitized
        letters, sermons, and manuscripts by and about Morais and makes them openly
        available for reading and research. Each item pairs a scholarly
        <abbr title="Text Encoding Initiative">TEI</abbr> transcription with
        high-resolution page images delivered through
        <abbr title="International Image Interoperability Framework">IIIF</abbr>, so
        readers can study the text and the original document side by side, with full
        descriptive metadata below.
      </p>

      <p>
        This repository is a project of
        <a href="https://judaicadhpenn.org/">Judaica Digital Humanities at Penn</a>,
        and is a companion to related collections including the
        <em>Gershwind-Bennett Isaac Leeser Digital Repository</em>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
